
				Variables
Variable
	• represents a memory location that is holding a single piece of data
	• in Java, variables have
		o a name
		o a type
	o a value

Variable Names
	• can start with
		o a letter ( a to z )
		o an underscore ( _ )
		o a dollar sign ( $ )
	• cannot be a reserved word
		o such as System, class
	• cannot be special operation symbols
	o such as +, -, %

Variable Type
	• the type of data held by the variable
	• two main types:
 
	primitive data type
		o holds a single number or character
 
	class data type
		o holds a class reference (methods and data)
		o will be discussed later in the course

Primitive Data Type
	• are built-in parts of Java
	• eight primitive types (variables)
		o for whole numbers
		o for floating point numbers
		o for binary decisions
		o for unicode letters

Variable Values
	• for whole numbers
		byte –128 to 127
		short –32,768 to 32,767
		int –2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647
		long very large

	• for decimal numbers
		float accurate to seven decimal places
		double accurate to fifteen decimal places
	• for binary decisions
		boolean true or false
	• for characters
		char any unicode letter
	• unicode
		o an encoding scheme
		o established by the Unicode Consortium
			www.unicode.org
		o allows the use of characters from any language

Variable Declarations
	• used to create a variable
		o specifies the type of data to be stored
		o gives a name to a memory location
		o assigns a value to the variable
	• example:
		int num = 10;
	o is set up to hold an integer
	o reserves a place in memory called num
	o holds the integer value 10

	• integer numbers:
		int iNumber = 34;
	• floating point:
		double aDouble = 2.0;
		float aFloat = 1.4f;
	• boolean:
		boolean isEmpty = true;
		boolean isReady = false;
	• char:
		char aChar = ‘x’; note: single quotations
	• can declare multiple variables in one line
		int aDigit = 3, aNumber = 44;

Constant Variable
	• cannot be changed after a program is compiled
	• to create use the final keyword
	• the variable name is usually capitalized
		final double RATE = 3.445;
		RATE = 10; //Error

Naming Convention
	• the variable name
	o is self-documenting and starts with a lower case letter
		 int sales; vs int x;
	o for 2 or more words the second and subsequent words can
		 start with a capital letter
			int totalSales
		 separated by an underscore
			int total_sales
